/-
Fmt/M1Ref.lean: the decision of the first toy (a product test; Fmt/M1All.lean) compared, at tiny
sizes, with a second reading of the same text, a reference program of this library's own in Python
(written independently; honest prover and verifier). The text is the programs' description of the
toy.
What the guards find:
  the honest run is accepted;
  the claims named under guard (2) below are the reference's, entry by entry;
  each round's first and last element sent and every word of every opened column bears on the
    decision.

Data and guards only (tests by Lean's evaluator; they leave no theorem). The data were put in by
program from the reference's trace of one run.
The sizes: K = 3; codes (6, 8, 3), (7, 4, 3), (8, 4, 3); mask codes (6, 16, 3), (7, 32, 3),
(8, 128, 3); three positions a list; 21 rounds. A code is a triple (a, l, r): codewords of length
2^a, messages of l entries, a tail of r entries (`Code`, Fmt/M1Codes.lean).
A block is one step of the toy's protocol, as the verifier's function of what was sent, drawn and
opened. BAT is the batching block, FOLD a fold, CS the code switch (the head of Fmt/M1Dec.lean).
The claim after a block is the linear equation that the block hands on (`GRow`, `SRow`,
Fmt/M1Types.lean).
The string is the function that runs all blocks one after the other (`decWith`, Fmt/M1Dec.lean).

Main names:
  `refPar`, `refView`: the sizes; the run as the decision sees it (elements sent, elements and
    positions drawn; the opened columns).
  `G1`, `S1`, `G2`, `S2`, `G3`: the claim after BAT, FOLD 1, CS 1, FOLD 2, CS 2, computed by this
    library's blocks from the view: written out directly in this file, with the rounds' numbers at
    these sizes. This is a copy of the string, not `decWith` itself. Guards (1) and (3) speak for
    `decWith`.

Guards.
  (1) `decM refPar refView`.
  (2) The four rows under BAT, and `G1`, `S1`, `G2`, `S2`, `G3`: coefficient vectors and targets
      equal to the reference's. So the order of every batching is the reference's too. An honest run
      alone is blind to that order.
  (3) Each round's first and last element sent made one larger: refused. Each word of each opened
      column made one larger: refused. A round less, a round more, a column less: refused.

What it shows: two independent readings of one text (the programs' description of the toy) agree, at
these sizes, in the claims named under (2). (They are apart in their writing; this is taken on
trust: the reference's functions for the verifier's state were written from the text alone, not from
a note or a file of this library's blocks.)
Limits: it does not show that either is what the programs (the C++ implementation that comes with
this Lean code) compute. This file's sources include no file or number of the programs, and nothing
of theirs is compared in it. (The decision's outcomes on the programs' own files are tested in
Fmt/M1FullProgPTa.lean, Fmt/M1FullProgPRa.lean, Fmt/M1FullProgPFa.lean and the files beside them.)
Both readings are of the same text, and that text had one writer.

Notation:
  K: a natural number, a field of `Par`; the rows a, b, c have 2^K entries (the head of
    Fmt/M1Types.lean);
  sizes ⟨fld, K, stages, work, salt⟩: the fields of `Par` (Fmt/M1Types.lean);
  `.two`: field 2, `Fld.two`, one of the format's two fields (`Fld`, Fmt/Rules1.lean);
  c, d, t: the fields of `GRow` (Fmt/M1Types.lean): coefficient vectors c and d and a target t of a
    linear equation (the head of Fmt/M1Psc.lean);
  c, d0, d1, t: fields of `SRow`: three coefficient vectors and the target of a linear equation (the
    head of Fmt/M1Types.lean).

Terms: lean/GLOSSARY.md.
-/
